WEBVTT

NOTE
SCAFFOLD — TIMINGS ARE NOT SYNCED TO THE AUDIO.

The cue text below is the §14 narration script, not a transcript of the current
recording. The timings are evenly apportioned placeholders. Do not enable
captions for patients until these are synced to the real audio.

Two ways to finish this:
  1. Re-record the intro to the script (the target is ~1:50, see Menu > Video
     script). Then the text is already correct and only the timings need
     adjusting to the new cut.
  2. Keep the current 3:44 recording and transcribe it, replacing the cue text
     with what is actually said.

Sync the timings by playing the video and noting the real in/out points, then
edit the timestamps here. Format is HH:MM:SS.mmm --> HH:MM:SS.mmm.

Once synced, flip the default in app.js: settings.captions = true in emptyData().

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
This app helps me see how your Parkinson's medicine is working through the day.
It takes a few seconds each time.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Every hour while you are awake, it will ask you one question:
how are you feeling right now?
You answer with one of three colours.

3
00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Red means you are off.
Stiff or frozen. Hard to get up from a chair or from bed.
Movements feel slow. Or a shaking tremor.
Your feet feel stuck to the floor, or you need help to stand up. That is red.

4
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Yellow means you are on.
You get up, walk and do all your work fairly easily.
You do not need anyone's help.
This is your good period. Most of the day should be yellow.

5
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Green means too much.
Extra movements you cannot control, getting in the way of what you are doing.
Your body, head or arms move on their own,
so eating or sitting still is difficult.

6
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:10.000
The app will also remind you at each tablet time.
Press "I have taken it" after you take the tablet, so I know the timing.

7
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Bring the phone to your next visit.
I will look at the chart and adjust your medicine.
